Create a highly detailed, practical, hour-by-hour itinerary for a short weekend trip from Dublin to any Schengen city in Europe.

The itinerary should be optimized for a 2-night / approximately 3-day city break, usually:

- Depart Dublin on Friday
- Return to Dublin on Sunday evening or late Sunday night
- Prefer direct flights
- Maximise useful time at the destination
- Avoid unnecessarily early Sunday return flights
- Prioritise a destination that can be experienced meaningfully in one weekend

Step 1 — Evaluate the destination and flights

Start by assessing whether the selected city is actually a good choice for a short weekend from Dublin.

Consider:

- Direct flight availability from Dublin
- Total return airfare
- Friday departure time
- Friday arrival time
- Sunday return time
- Airport-to-city transfer time
- Amount of usable time at the destination
- Typical or forecast weather
- Hotel prices
- Walkability
- Amount of sightseeing possible in 2–3 days
- Food and restaurant quality
- Whether the city is better suited to a longer trip
- Overall value for money

If several flight combinations exist, identify the best flight combination for maximising the weekend, not simply the cheapest fare.

A slightly more expensive flight is acceptable if it provides substantially more usable time.

Confirmed travel information

I will provide some or all of the following:

- Destination
- Travel dates
- Airline
- Flight numbers
- Dublin departure time
- Destination arrival time
- Return departure time
- Dublin arrival time
- Flight price
- Hotel
- Hotel address
- Hotel price
- Check-in/check-out times
- Whether breakfast is included

Treat supplied booking details as confirmed.

If something is not yet booked, clearly identify it as pending rather than assuming it is confirmed.

Trip style

This is primarily a sightseeing and food-focused European city break.

Prioritise:

- Major must-see attractions
- Historic centre / Old Town
- Beautiful architecture
- Important historical sites
- Culture
- Photogenic locations
- Scenic viewpoints
- Riverfronts, canals, lakes or waterfront areas where relevant
- Local neighbourhoods with character
- Local food
- Regional cuisine
- Good cafés
- Atmospheric evening experiences
- Walking
- Efficient routes
- Enough relaxed time to actually experience the city

Avoid:

- Spending large portions of the weekend commuting
- Low-priority attractions
- Tourist traps unless genuinely iconic
- Attractions requiring half a day unless they're exceptional
- Excessive museum time unless the city is particularly known for them
- Packing every 30 minutes with activities
- Day trips that undermine the city-break experience

Sightseeing

Identify the essential attractions for a first visit.

Classify them as:

- ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Essential
- ⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strongly recommended
- ⭐⭐⭐ Worth doing if time permits
- Optional

Design each day's sightseeing geographically so nearby attractions are grouped together.

For every major attraction include:

- Recommended visit time
- Approximate duration
- Why it is worth seeing
- Current opening hours
- Current ticket price
- Whether advance booking is necessary
- Best time of day
- Google Maps link

Where appropriate, include:

- Sunrise/sunset viewpoints
- Best photography spots
- Historic walking routes
- Markets
- Cathedrals/churches
- Castles/palaces
- Main squares
- Local neighbourhoods
- River or waterfront walks
- Parks/gardens
- Observation decks
- UNESCO sites

Food

Include recommendations for every useful meal during the trip.

Normally include:

Friday

- Optional café/snack after arrival
- Dinner

Saturday

- Breakfast if not included
- Coffee stop
- Lunch
- Afternoon coffee/dessert if appropriate
- Dinner

Sunday

- Breakfast if not included
- Lunch
- Coffee/dessert
- Early or final dinner before the airport

Prioritise local and regional cuisine rather than generic international restaurants.

For every restaurant/café provide:

- Name
- Area
- Why it is worth visiting
- Recommended dishes
- Approximate price level
- Current opening hours
- Whether reservation is recommended
- Ideal reservation time
- Google Maps link

Avoid recommending the same type of food for every meal.

Include at least one signature local dish or food experience.

Transport

Explain exactly how to get around.

Prioritise:

1. Walking
2. Metro/tram/bus when useful
3. Bolt/Uber/taxi when it meaningfully saves time

For public transport explain:

- Ticket types
- Current ticket prices
- Whether tickets are time-based or journey-based
- Where to buy them
- Whether contactless bank cards can be used
- Whether tickets must be validated
- Whether the city supports tap-on/tap-off
- Whether Apple Pay/Google Pay works
- Whether a day pass or multi-day pass makes financial sense for this itinerary

Do not recommend a transport pass automatically. Compare its cost with likely individual journeys first.

Airport transfers

Provide detailed instructions for:

Arrival

Airport → hotel

Departure

Hotel/city → airport

For each direction compare:

- Train
- Metro
- Bus
- Airport express
- Taxi
- Bolt/Uber where available

Include:

- Current fare
- Typical journey time
- Frequency
- Where to board
- Where to buy the ticket
- Google Maps route

Recommend the most sensible option based on the trip schedule.

For the return journey, build in a comfortable airport buffer.

Hotel considerations

Assess the hotel's location for:

- Airport transport
- Walkability
- Historic centre access
- Restaurants
- Public transport
- Safety
- Sunday luggage storage

If checkout is much earlier than the flight, build the itinerary around leaving luggage with the hotel or an alternative luggage-storage service.

Include a Google Maps link for the hotel.

